by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-18T15:01:15Z

Thanks for the answer on the id semantics, that unblocked everything. I took over the rework to get this into 8.2; here is what changed, so you can review rather than redo:

- v2 is now the primary code path and v1 is isolated in `doSendLegacyApi()`/`getLegacyPayload()`, kept byte-for-byte at its 7.3 behavior. Since AhaSend has v1 in maintenance mode, sending through it now triggers a deprecation, and the legacy code can be deleted in 9.0.
- The version is selected by the presence of the account id in the DSN instead of sniffing the key prefix. The prefix is still checked for one thing: a `aha-sk-` key without an account id fails at build time with a message giving the right DSN form. A hypothetical v1 key with that prefix can no longer break a working install.
- `$accountId` moved to the last constructor position: inserting it before `$client` broke every positional caller of `new AhaSendApiTransport($key, $client)`.
- The `ApiVersion` enum is gone; it was public API that nothing outside the transport used.
- Multi-recipient sends: v2 returns one entry per recipient, so the transport now dispatches an `AhaSendDeliveryEvent` per rejected recipient and records the first returned `Message-ID` (same convention as the Mandrill, Infobip, Scaleway and Mailjet bridges). `testSendV2` now mocks one entry per recipient, which is what the API actually returns.
- Tags go through the native v2 `tags` field instead of the `AhaSend-Tags` header.
- The converter rejects payloads missing `message_id_header` or `recipient` with a `ParseException` (406) instead of an uncaught `TypeError`; new `AhaSendPayloadConverterTest` covers the id switch and the guards, and I verified both fail on the previous code.
- Squashed to one commit under your authorship, rebased on current 8.2, CHANGELOG/UPGRADE/README aligned.

The webhook id switch stays unconditional, as you noted it must be: the converter cannot know which API sent the mail, and the internal id joins to nothing anyway. It is now called out in the changelog and description as a behavior change instead of "(v2 support only)".
